WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.804
TypeScript database libraries offer
several abstractions:

2
00:00:03.884 --> 00:00:06.679
driver-level SQL with parameters, query
builders,

3
00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:10.826
object-relational mappers, tagged-template
SQL, and stored procedures.

4
00:00:10.906 --> 00:00:15.226
Their normal structured APIs often bind
values safely because callers describe

5
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:16.958
fields and values separately.

6
00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:20.087
That advantage is real, but it is not a
blanket guarantee.

7
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:24.551
The security result depends on the exact
method and on whether application data is

8
00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:26.664
treated as a value or inserted as text.

9
00:00:26.744 --> 00:00:31.065
Most mature libraries expose escape
hatches for queries their structured model

10
00:00:31.145 --> 00:00:32.034
cannot express.

11
00:00:32.114 --> 00:00:36.689
Names such as raw, unsafe, literal,
fragment, or expression deserve focused review.

12
00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:41.026
A raw method may still support parameter
arrays and be safe when used correctly,

13
00:00:41.106 --> 00:00:45.617
while an unsafe interpolation helper can
convert its argument into SQL source text.

14
00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:48.937
Tagged templates require the same semantic
understanding:

15
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:51.749
a well-designed tag can bind interpolated
values,

16
00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:56.086
but an ordinary JavaScript template
literal or a special raw-fragment wrapper

17
00:00:56.166 --> 00:00:57.754
performs text construction.

18
00:00:57.834 --> 00:01:01.392
Syntax that looks similar can have a
different security contract.

19
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:05.729
ORM-generated operations can still be
insecure for reasons beyond injection,

20
00:01:05.809 --> 00:01:07.397
such as missing tenant scope,

21
00:01:07.477 --> 00:01:12.115
and injection risk returns when developers
build predicates or raw clauses manually.

22
00:01:12.195 --> 00:01:14.673
Stored procedures follow the same
principle.

23
00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:18.120
Calling a procedure with bound arguments
may protect the call,

24
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.394
yet a procedure that concatenates those
arguments into dynamic SQL can remain

25
00:01:22.474 --> 00:01:24.253
vulnerable inside the database.

26
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.382
Abstraction changes where the boundary is
implemented;

27
00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.178
it does not remove the boundary.

28
00:01:29.258 --> 00:01:30.528
For every database API,

29
00:01:30.608 --> 00:01:35.119
consult its documentation and determine
whether the value is bound or inserted into

30
00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:36.089
the SQL program.

31
00:01:36.169 --> 00:01:40.553
Prefer the structured, parameterized path
and isolate necessary raw SQL behind a

32
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.094
small reviewed interface.

33
00:01:42.174 --> 00:01:46.050
Make that interface accept a fixed query
or typed operation plus values,

34
00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.243
rather than an arbitrary complete fragment
from callers.

35
00:01:49.323 --> 00:01:53.770
This reduces the number of places where
reviewers must reason about SQL provenance

36
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.980
and makes a later migration or library
upgrade less likely to change security

37
00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.140
behavior silently.
